**Overall verdict: OVERCLAIMED.** T, W, DB, and the leaf-output version of GR-F are sound. They establish useful restrictions on specified observables, not the absence of a classical Fourier mechanism. The main failures are GR-F’s unrestricted corollary, BASIS’s “iff,” SM’s nonexistence inference, Q1(d), and PF’s complexity conclusion.

Throughout, distinguish absolute power \(A_j=|\widehat F(j)|^2\) from relative power \(A_j/\operatorname{Var}(F)\). The note switches between them.

**T — CORRECT.**

Fourier inversion on \(G\), followed by restriction to \(H\), gives
\[
g(a^t)=\sum_{\chi\in\widehat G}\widehat g(\chi)\chi(a)^t.
\]
Orthogonality gives the identity. Every character of \(H\) has exactly \(i\) extensions; Cauchy–Schwarz gives the stated bounds. Indeed, the order of the restriction **divides** the order of every extension.

This is the standard restriction formula in finite abelian harmonic analysis. “Only characters concentrate” is acceptable shorthand for a necessary condition, but not an algorithmic characterization:

- The factor \(i\) can make the conclusion weak.
- Extension coefficients can cancel. If \(\lambda|_H=1\), then \(g=(\chi-\chi\lambda)/2\) vanishes on \(H\), despite substantial character correlations.

Thus T supplies no converse and no efficient way to identify the relevant characters.

**W — CORRECT, with the numerical assertion requiring reproducible inputs.**

For unit \(h\),
\[
\sum_{y\in H}\overline{\eta_j(y)}e(hy/N)
=\frac1i\sum_{\chi|_H=\eta_j}
\sum_{y\in G}\overline{\chi(y)}e(hy/N).
\]
Each complete sum factors into prime-modulus Gauss sums, each bounded by the square root of its prime modulus. This proves the bound.

For the shifted Jacobi observable, \(c\in G\) is essential. Local complete sums are Jacobi sums; their exceptional character cases have magnitude \(1\), rather than producing an exceptional large frequency. The claimed bound survives.

Interval completion gives the MSB bound. Nonunit additive frequencies require separate treatment; their larger complete sums are compensated by their smaller interval Fourier coefficients.

My small-case computation gives a near-sharp example:
\[
N=143,\quad a=123,\quad h=142,\quad r=60,\quad j=58,
\]
with \(|\widehat F(j)|/(\sqrt N/r)=0.999547\).

These are classical Gauss/Jacobi-sum arguments. Relevant stronger subgroup-cancellation literature is [Bourgain–Glibichuk–Konyagin, *Estimates for the Number of Sums and Products and for Exponential Sums in Fields of Prime Order*, JLMS 73 (2006), 380–398](https://doi.org/10.1112/S0024610706022721). Its prime-field results do not classify arbitrary computable observables modulo \(pq\).

**HNP — CORRECT WITH FIXES.**

The change of Fourier geometry is correct: under your convention,
\[
\widehat{b(\alpha\,\cdot)}(k)=\widehat b(k\alpha^{-1}),
\]
so additive concentration moves to frequencies \(\alpha k\). Exponentiation instead pulls back the multiplicative spectrum.

However, Akavia’s actual oracle is \(P(sg^a)\), indexed by exponents. Her advice contains **discrete logs of the prescribed additive SFT query points**, allowing access to \(x\mapsto P(sx)\). This access conversion is an essential part of the explanation, not merely a distinction between two homomorphisms. See [Akavia, *Solving Hidden Number Problem with One Bit Oracle and Advice*, CRYPTO 2009, LNCS 5677, 337–354, §3.1, Theorem 1](https://people.csail.mit.edu/akavia/akaviaCrypto2009.pdf).

Also, “multiplicatively flat” must retain the quantitative bound and its dependence on \(r\). W becomes uninformative for sufficiently short orbits.

The learning machinery is known: [Goldreich–Levin, STOC 1989, 25–32](https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~oded/annot/node43.html); [Kushilevitz–Mansour, *Learning Decision Trees Using the Fourier Spectrum*, SIAM J. Comput. 22 (1993), 1331–1348](https://doi.org/10.1137/0222080); [Gilbert–Muthukrishnan–Strauss, *Improved Time Bounds for Near-Optimal Sparse Fourier Representations*, SPIE 5914, 59141A (2005)](https://doi.org/10.1117/12.615931); and [Akavia’s 2008 MIT thesis, Chapter 3, especially Theorem 3.16](https://people.csail.mit.edu/akavia/AkaviaPhDThesis.pdf). Her [COLT 2010 paper, 381–393, Theorem 1](https://www.learningtheory.org/colt2010/papers/005akavia.pdf), gives deterministic SFT with an additional Fourier-\(\ell^1\) bound.

**GR-F — CORRECT WITH FIXES; the unrestricted corollary is false.**

For a terminating deterministic tree with fixed complex leaf labels, the proof works, including adaptive branching within an evaluation. Before the first split, all three executions follow the same path; outside \(S\), they reach the same labelled leaf. Hence
\[
\|F-F_p\|_2^2,\ \|F-F_q\|_2^2\le4\varepsilon.
\]
Writing \(A_p=(r/r_p)\mathbb Z/r\) and \(A_q=(r/r_q)\mathbb Z/r\), Parseval gives
\[
\sum_{j\notin A_p\cap A_q}|\widehat F(j)|^2
\le \|F-F_p\|_2^2+\|F-F_q\|_2^2\le8\varepsilon.
\]
Their intersection is indeed \((r/g)\mathbb Z/r\).

The interpretation is arithmetically right:
\[
j=k(r/g)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\operatorname{ord}(\eta_j)=g/\gcd(g,k).
\]
But **divisors of \(g\) need not be small, smooth, or useless**. The theorem leaves all common-order information unrestricted.

A decisive example is \(N=77,\ a=-1\). Use one test \(y-1=0\), returning \(+1\) on equality and \(-1\) otherwise. Then
\[
r_p=r_q=r=g=2,\qquad \varepsilon=0,\qquad F(t)=(-1)^t.
\]
There is a unit-power nonzero peak, with no splits. Thus “generic observables give no useful Fourier information unless they split” does not follow.

Further qualifications:

- **Randomness:** fix and couple the random tape across the three executions. The inequality then holds averaged over tapes; Jensen also bounds the spectrum of the expected observable.
- **Computed-value outputs:** excluded. Allowing arbitrary decoding of a computed residue destroys the theorem. For \(N=15,a=2,\Psi(y)=e(y/15)\), no tests occur, yet the mass outside the common frequencies is exactly \(5/8\).
- **Extensions and curves:** require a specified CRT-compatible representation and operations. An extension-ring split is detected through coordinate gcds; “\(\gcd(v,N)\)” is otherwise undefined. Representation-dependent output decoding remains excluded.
- **Across-query adaptivity:** does not automatically produce one fixed observable. Condition on history and state the sampling model.

Uniform independent evaluations find a split in expected \(O(1/\varepsilon)\) evaluations, monitoring gcds at every test. This is an available factoring algorithm, **not a proved lower bound on every Fourier strategy**. Evaluation size and monitoring costs must be counted.

The generic CRT-simulation method has substantial precedent: [Aggarwal–Maurer, *Breaking RSA Generically is Equivalent to Factoring*, EUROCRYPT 2009, 36–53](https://crypto.ethz.ch/publications/AggMau09.html). Especially relevant is [Jager–Schwenk, *On the Analysis of Cryptographic Assumptions in the Generic Ring Model*, ASIACRYPT 2009, 399–416](https://eprint.iacr.org/2009/621): even Jacobi-symbol computation becomes factoring-equivalent generically, although it is easy with ordinary integer representations. Your spectral packaging may merit recording; a new general hardness barrier has not been established.

**DB — CORRECT.**

For \(0<\rho<1\) and \(j\ne0\),
\[
|\widehat F(j)|\le \rho,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(F)=\rho(1-\rho).
\]
The complement also gives \(|\widehat F(j)|\le1-\rho\), yielding the stronger symmetric bound
\[
\frac{|\widehat F(j)|^2}{\operatorname{Var}(F)}
\le\min\!\left\{\frac{\rho}{1-\rho},\frac{1-\rho}{\rho}\right\}.
\]
This is elementary and known. The conclusion concerns **superpolynomially small** densities; inverse-polynomial rarity still permits inverse-polynomial relative peaks.

**SM — OVERCLAIMED.**

A simultaneous-approximation argument supplies a nontrivial character with small phases on the specified unit primes, with the stated scale. But it does not ensure:

1. large character order;
2. a nontrivial restriction to \(H\);
3. efficient construction.

A character annihilating \(H\) only produces the DC coefficient.

If a suitable restriction is nontrivial and
\[
|\arg\chi(y)|\le 2\pi\delta\Omega(y)\ll1
\]
on the smooth orbit representatives, then directly
\[
|\widehat F(j)|\gtrsim\rho.
\]
Therefore absolute power is approximately \(\rho^2\), while relative power is approximately \(\rho/(1-\rho)\). The reported “\(0.3–0.5\,\rho\)” must mean **relative** power; for small \(\rho\), that value as absolute power contradicts DB.

The density calculation \(u\log u=O(\log n)\) for inverse-polynomial smoothness density is a reasonable Dickman-model estimate. It makes the pigeonhole guarantee ineffective because \(\pi(B)\) becomes enormous. **An ineffective existence guarantee is not a nonexistence theorem.** The proposed equidistribution of character values needs a precise conjecture, including their dependencies.

[Granville–Soundararajan, *Large Character Sums: Pretentious Characters and the Pólya–Vinogradov Theorem*, JAMS 20 (2007), 357–384](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0503113), provides relevant pretentious-character theory, not the claimed smoothness-observable barrier.

The finite data cannot establish domination by relation collection. “Primal versus dual” is a useful analogy only after defining \(L\); Shor samples annihilator information, and the analogy supplies no complexity comparison.

**UP — CORRECT WITH FIXES; a stronger elementary statement holds.**

Your inequalities establish \(m>r/2\), retaining the strict inequality. But strict Fourier diagonal dominance actually requires
\[
\boxed{m=r}.
\]
If \(w(x_*)=0\), Fourier inversion gives
\[
\widehat w(j_0)e(j_0x_*/r)
=-\sum_{j\ne j_0}\widehat w(j)e(jx_*/r),
\]
contradicting strict dominance.

Thus the stated hypothesis is meaningful but extremely demanding: one coefficient must dominate every adversarial phase alignment. Actual modular-exponentiation signals do not provide arbitrary independent phases. The conclusion does not cover nonlinear statistics, adaptive identification, approximate cancellation, or distributional success.

The uncertainty framework is classical: [Donoho–Stark, *Uncertainty Principles and Signal Recovery*, SIAM J. Appl. Math. 49 (1989), 906–931](https://doi.org/10.1137/0149053). [Tao, *An Uncertainty Principle for Cyclic Groups of Prime Order*, MRL 12 (2005), 121–127](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0308286), concerns support sizes and prime cyclic groups; it is not this amplitude-dominance theorem.

The \(r/\log r\) sampling observation needs a signal ensemble, estimator, and success criterion. It is not a universal lower bound. [Aaronson, *BQP and the Polynomial Hierarchy*, STOC 2010, §4](https://www.scottaaronson.com/papers/bqpph.pdf), establishes Fourier Fishing hardness in an oracle setting, not for these explicit arithmetic signals.

**BASIS — OVERCLAIMED.**

For the ideal state \(r^{-1/2}\sum_t|t\rangle|a^t\rangle\),
\[
w_k=\frac1r\sum_t|\beta_k(a^t)|^2,\qquad
|\phi_k\rangle=
\frac{\sum_t\overline{\beta_k(a^t)}|t\rangle}{\sqrt{r w_k}}.
\]
These identities and Schmidt rank \(r\) are correct. An arbitrary basis expansion is generally **not** a Schmidt decomposition.

The algorithmic “iff” fails. T is one-way, contains cancellation, and concerns unnormalised coefficients. One must additionally provide efficient branch sampling, amplitude access, normalisation, and enough frequency-order information to recover \(r\).

The character expansion requires the normalising factor \(1/\sqrt r\). It still has \(r\) terms, and the rank remains \(r\). Qubit-product assertions also depend on register conventions.

Faithful, accurately labelled character evaluation can expose discrete logs; low-order character evaluation need not. “Entries are characters, i.e. discrete logs” is too broad.

Nearby established simulation results are [Van den Nest, *Simulating Quantum Computers with Probabilistic Methods*, QIC 11 (2011), 784–812](https://www.rintonpress.com/xxqic11/qic-11-910/0784-0812.pdf), including a condition on classical postprocessing spectra, and [Schwarz–Van den Nest, *Simulating Quantum Circuits with Sparse Output Distributions* (2013), arXiv:1310.6749](https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.6749). Their explicit tractability and sparsity assumptions cannot be replaced by a choice of basis.

**Q1(a) — CORRECT.**

For Blum \(N\), the 2-primary subgroup is \(C_2\times C_2\). Thus
\[
v_2(r)=0\iff a\text{ is square modulo both primes}.
\]
Otherwise \(v_2(r)=1\). This is the standard elementary QR equivalence; Jacobi-\(-1\) inputs are already decidable.

**Q1(b) — CORRECT.**

For \(N\equiv1\pmod3\), excluding the impossible factor \(3\), the primes are either both \(1\) or both \(2\pmod3\). In the latter case every answer is false. In the former, writing \(\alpha=v_3(p-1)\), \(\beta=v_3(q-1)\),
\[
\Pr[3\mid\operatorname{ord}_N(a)]
=1-3^{-\alpha-\beta}\ge8/9.
\]
Repeated independent samples decide the property with bounded error. It is equivalent to \(-3\) being square modulo both primes.

Computed examples: the probabilities for \(N=91,133,55,187\) are respectively \(8/9,26/27,0,0\). This proves the stated reduction, not a factoring equivalence.

**Q1(c) — CORRECT WITH FIXES.**

With \(s=p+q\), both identities follow from
\[
N\equiv s-1\pmod{\lambda(N)}.
\]
Balance places \(s\) in an interval of width \(O(\sqrt N)\), allowing an \(O(N^{1/4})\) interval-DL search. However, a returned exponent need only equal \(s\) modulo \(r\); it need not identify the actual sum.

The Cheon sentence is inaccurate. Standard auxiliary-input attacks include divisors of a known prime group order **minus or plus one**, with different auxiliary-input requirements. See [Cheon, *Discrete Logarithm Problems with Auxiliary Inputs*, Journal of Cryptology 23 (2010), 457–476](https://www.math.snu.ac.kr/~jhcheon/publications/2010/StrongDH_JoC_Final2.pdf), extending EUROCRYPT 2006. No applicable speedup is established here, but the proposed universal prerequisite is wrong.

**Q1(d) — FALSE as written.**

There is no stated model supporting this classification of “computable.”

Concrete counterexample: modulo \(35\), inflate a cubic character modulo \(7\). It is not generated by the Jacobi symbol modulo \(35\): the cubic character can take value \(\zeta_3\) at \(3\), whereas \((3/35)=1\).

Moreover, no Eisenstein integer has norm \(35\), because \(5\) is inert and must occur to an even exponent in a norm. Thus an order-\(3\) character does **not** require an element of norm \(N\); a denominator above the factor \(7\) suffices.

If the intended restriction is “uniform polynomial time, given only an unfactored cryptographic modulus,” say so. The asserted classification then remains unsupported, rather than becoming a theorem. Higher reciprocity computations with supplied algebraic denominators are standard; see [Bach–Sandlund, *On Euclidean Methods for Cubic and Quartic Jacobi Symbols* (2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.07719).

Cancellation of rational-input symbols at the rational ideal \((N)\) in appropriate higher-residue settings does not establish uniqueness of Jacobi. Nor is any unconditional theorem supplied forcing every efficiently evaluable character to have polynomially smooth order.

**Q3 — OVERCLAIMED.**

The displayed equivalence is correct:
\[
r_p\mid N^e-1\iff q^e\equiv1\pmod{r_p},
\]
because \(p\equiv1\pmod{r_p}\).

But “all special elements” is an undefined class, and the proposed dichotomy is not proved. Lucas, torus, and elliptic-curve methods require their actual group orders and success conditions to be analysed.

In the dual-number ring, write \(1+t\), \(t^2=0\). Then \((1+t)^k=1+kt\), so its order is exactly \(N\). This elementary fact changes the group and provides no recovery of the original \(r\).

**PF — FALSE as a blanket statement.**

The justified bounds, for their respective classes, are
\[
A_j\le8\varepsilon\quad((r/g)\nmid j),\qquad
A_j\le B^2N/r^2,\qquad
A_j\le\rho^2.
\]
Common-order peaks must remain exempt; the \(N=77\) example shows why. Jacobi-generated observables can also have a unit-power order-two peak. “Nontrivial” must therefore specify an order threshold, not merely \(j\ne0\).

For relative thresholds, the first two bounds require division by \(\operatorname{Var}(F)\). A general “Weil-class” additionally needs explicit twist exceptions.

An output-list guarantee gives an **upper bound** \(O(1/\tau)\) on list size after energy normalisation. Output-size lower bounds require a family actually having \(\Theta(1/\tau)\) mandatory outputs; they are not instancewise lower bounds for every observable class.

Finally, ordinary SFT assumes a known Fourier domain. Here \(r\) is unknown. A reduction must specify a known sampling window, frequency resolution, and reconstruction procedure.

[Cleve, *The Query Complexity of Order-Finding*, Information and Computation 192 (2004), 162–171](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9911124), proves an exponential classical lower bound for a permutation-power oracle model. It does not establish an arithmetic lower bound for modular exponentiation.

My reproducible [checks](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/periodfind_2/referee_checks.py) include T, 276 density cases, 1,248 Gauss/Jacobi cases, and 150 adaptive trees.

## strongest objection to the verdict

The strongest objection to my negative overall verdict is that the core mathematics survives substantial scrutiny: GR-F is valid in its stated leaf-output model, W and DB exclude important observable families, and UP can be strengthened. These are worthwhile results.

None of my counterexamples supplies a new polynomial-time factoring algorithm. They show that the note should claim **conditional obstructions for specified observables and access models**, while leaving the search for an efficiently evaluable observable with useful large-order concentration open.